# Files loaded in the order of the list. Separator = backslash, never a slash.
# Core/Locale.lua is listed FIRST among the Core/ modules: the language layer is
# a dependency of Config, Intermission, Panel and UI.
# Core/Sound.lua comes right AFTER Locale.lua and BEFORE Config.lua: it owns the
# pure table "canonical state -> soundboard file", the one-playback-per-assignment
# gate and the BOUNDED preference resolved by Config.resolveIntermission
# (/gr sound on|off). It depends on nothing else, so it stays out of the way of
# the module order below.
# Core/Simulation.lua comes AFTER Core/Intermission.lua (it reuses the canonical
# states, the ping labels and the binding candidates) and BEFORE the UI/ files.
# Core/Layout.lua comes LAST among the Core/ modules: it computes the DISPOSITION
# of the panels (pure geometry, measured from Core/Locale strings) and the UI/
# files apply it as-is.
# Bindings.xml is NOT listed here: the client loads it automatically
# (https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Creating_key_bindings).
GideonRaid.lua
Core\Locale.lua
Core\Sound.lua
Core\Config.lua
Core\Pairing.lua
Core\Intermission.lua
Core\Simulation.lua
Core\Layout.lua
UI\Panel.lua
UI\Intermission.lua

# SOUNDBOARDS OF AN ASSIGNMENT (one file per canonical state, played ONCE when
# the player declares their composition). THEY MUST BE LISTED HERE: the client
# does NOT load a sound file that is not listed in the .toc, and an unlisted file
# makes PlaySoundFile fail silently.
# These three files are SILENT PLACEHOLDERS (0.2 s of silence, Ogg Vorbis) until
# the raid lead delivers the real recordings. REPLACING THEM IS A FILE DROP:
# same names, same folder (Sound/), same format (Ogg Vorbis), no code change -
# see README.md, section "Replacing the three sounds".
Sound\assign-1v3r.ogg
Sound\assign-2v2r.ogg
Sound\assign-3v1r.ogg
